@@ -0,0 +1,108 @@
+# Symbolica bug reproducers from the HyperLica audit
+
+Baseline: `0b57776bf911faeea7e28ea133706fb03740ffeb` (Symbolica `dev`).
+These standalone examples use APIs already present at that revision; they do
+not import HyperLica. Supply any required `SYMBOLICA_LICENSE` through the
+environment. No license is included in this repository.
+
+Run from the Symbolica checkout, using a debug build for the factorization
+assertion. Each example asserts its expected result, rather than merely
+printing an expression. The fixed cases are also covered by library tests.
+
+```sh
+cargo run --locked --no-default-features --features integer-gmp,float-mpfr \
+  --example mre_factored_construction -- all
+cargo run --locked --no-default-features --features integer-gmp,float-mpfr \
+  --example mre_constant_rearrangement
+cargo run --locked --no-default-features --features integer-gmp,float-mpfr \
+  --example mre_constant_rearrangement -- shrink
+cargo run --locked --no-default-features --features integer-gmp,float-mpfr \
+  --example mre_sparse_partial_fractions
+```
+
+## Fixed constructor and arithmetic cases
+
+Select one case with `--example mre_factored_construction -- CASE`; `--list`
+prints the available names. `all` runs the eight groups below. The denominator
+is supplied as explicit `(base, multiplicity)` entries. Powered cancellation
+and rational scaling enable factorization; zero-denominator rejection checks
+both modes, and the other cases disable it. Values are checked against ordinary rational-polynomial
+arithmetic, independent of the factored constructor under test.
+
+| Case | Minimal condition | Baseline defect |
+|---|---|---|
+| `powered_cancellation` | `v / v^2` | Cancels the entire powered denominator, yielding `1` instead of `1/v`. |
+| `negative_sign` | `1 / (-v)^2` | Applies a negative leading unit only once instead of twice. |
+| `rational_scaling` | `(1/2) / (v/2)^2` | Loses the scalar from clearing rational denominator coefficients. |
+| `finite_field_units` | `1 / (3*v)^2` over GF(17) | Uses the leading unit to the wrong power (`6/v^2` instead of `2/v^2`). |
+| `algebraic_units` | A powered non-monic base over an algebraic field | Same leading-unit multiplicity error. |
+| `zero_denominator` | A positive-power zero denominator over Z | Accepts an invalid denominator, or discards it when factoring. |
+| `zero_power` | The removable denominator entry `(0, 0)` over GF(17) | Accesses the leading coefficient of an empty polynomial. |
+| `duplicate_arithmetic` | `a=1/(v*v)`, `b=1/v` with explicit duplicate bases | Duplicate entries violate downstream Add/Mul/Div assumptions. |
+
+The last group can be selected individually as `duplicate_add`, `duplicate_mul`
+or `duplicate_div`. The patched constructors merge equal bases after sign/unit
+normalization, without requiring polynomial factorization. The zero-denominator
+case catches and checks the intentional rejection panic; its panic-hook output
+does not indicate a failed example when the process exits successfully.
+
+## Fixed context and partial-fraction cases
+
+`mre_constant_rearrangement` grows a constant's empty variable map to `[x]`;
+`shrink` performs the reverse operation. Zero, integer, and non-integral rational
+constants retain their value and requested map. The baseline reaches a
+zero-sized slice-chunk operation and panics.
+
+`mre_sparse_partial_fractions` decomposes
+`1 + 1/(x+1)^3 + 1/(x+1) + 1/(x+2)` with variable map `[x, unused]`.
+There is no order-two term at `x=-1`. The baseline emits a spurious zero term
+with a lost variable map. The patched operation omits that term; the example
+also requires preservation of the original variable identities and exact
+reconstruction of the input.
+
+## Unresolved upstream diagnostics
+
+These two examples intentionally preserve failures. They are not fixes and
+must not be treated as passing smoke tests. Both failures were observed on
+pre-audit artifacts as well as the patched source.
+
+```sh
+RUST_BACKTRACE=1 cargo run --locked --no-default-features \
+  --features integer-gmp,float-mpfr --example mre_galois_upgrade -- 100
+cargo run --locked --no-default-features --features integer-gmp,float-mpfr \
+  --example mre_root_interval_expectation
+```
+
+`mre_galois_upgrade` is a bounded, fixed-input reproducer of an intermittent
+factorization panic over GF(2), in the existing Galois-upgrade test case.
+An internal call to `quot_rem_univariate_monic` receives a non-monic divisor
+over an algebraic extension. It prints the attempt number and checks exact
+reconstruction and the factor count on successful attempts. Randomized choices
+mean a successful run does not disprove the issue; rerun in a fresh process.
+Pre-audit debug artifacts failed on attempts 5 and 2 in independent runs with
+a 100-attempt limit. No factorization production code was changed in this audit.
+
+`mre_root_interval_expectation` isolates the existing degree-38 test input and
+checks its fifth interval. Both revisions produce `[3/16, 9/32]` where the test
+expects `[15/64, 9/32]`. Refinement to `[1023/4096, 2049/8192]` agrees. This is a
+focused standalone reproducer of a **test-expectation mismatch**, not evidence
+of an incorrect root, and the polynomial has not been minimized further.
+
+The frozen native audit gate passed 625 of 627 tests, with these two failures.
+Subsequently added duplicate-base regressions passed in the focused 21-test
+constructor/partial-fraction gate. The final combined full-library rerun passed
+629 of 630 tests: the interval expectation still failed, while the randomized
+factorization happened to pass in that run. The latter remains unresolved.
+Run with a C++ compiler on PATH for the native-code-generation tests; an earlier
+rerun without it had an additional environment-only failure. Do not report the
+full native suite as green.
+
+## Checking the unpatched revision
+
+Create a separate worktree at the baseline above and copy only the five
+`examples/mre_*.rs` files into its `examples` directory. Run the same commands
+there with a separate Cargo target directory. Do not copy patched library
+sources: the examples deliberately require no newly added API. Constructor
+cases can be run individually so the first assertion does not mask later bugs.
+Keep the checkout path, revision, features, compiler version and complete
+backtrace with any upstream issue report.
